  |publisher = {{ill|World Encyclopedia|ja|世界大百科事典}} 2nd Edition / Kotobank 
  |accessdate = 2018-03-07 }}
</ref> Larger-scale examples may be defined as {{ill|religious cities|ja|宗教都市}} or {{ill|precinct cities|ja|境内都市}}. Historically, sites for urban formation included castles, ports, markets, and exchange hubs, with temples and shrines being among these.

Monzenmachi typically developed in front of prominent shrines or temples that attracted many worshippers, creating hubs for commerce and crafts to cater to both shrine officials and visitors. However, such towns were not always formed even with large temples, as some temples restricted public access.<ref>{{Cite web|和書|url=https://www.worldtimes.co.jp/japan/20221119-166722/ |title=Comprehensive repair of thatched roofs after 70 years: The national important cultural property "Main Hall of Jionji" in Sagae City, Yamagata Prefecture |publisher=The World Times |date=2022-11-19 |accessdate=2023-05-26}}</ref>

Districts retaining historical features as Monzenmachi are designated as {{ill|Important Preservation Districts for Groups of Traditional Buildings|ja|重要伝統的建造物群保存地区}} and preserved accordingly. Exchanges between Monzenmachi towns have also taken place, with the {{ill|National Monzenmachi Summit|ja|全国門前町サミット}} being held annually since 1998.

