{{Otheruses|A type of historical town|A former municipality in Hōsu District, Ishikawa Prefecture, now part of Wajima City|Monzen Town (Ishikawa)|Other uses|Monzen Town (disambiguation)}}
{{Cite sources clearly|date=June 8, 2015 (Mon) 05:51 (UTC)}}
[[File:Sakamoto Otsu03s4592.jpg|thumb|Sakamoto]]
[[File:Toriimoto03s3200.jpg|thumb|Sagatoriimoto]]
[[File:Stone stairway Kiyomizu-dera.JPG|thumb|150px|[[Sannenzaka]]]]
'''Monzen Town''' (門前町, monzen-machi) refers to a town formed around prominent {{ill|Buddhist temples|en|寺院}} or {{ill|Shinto shrines|en|神社}}. Specifically, for shrines, it is often called a '''Torii-mae Town''' (鳥居前町, torii-mae-machi).

== Overview ==

In a broad sense, the term includes settlements formed by shrine or temple followers, such as shaji towns and {{ill|Jinaimachi|en|寺内町}}.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://kotobank.jp/word/%E9%96%80%E5%89%8D%E7%94%BA-143199#E4.B8.96.E7.95.8C.E5.A4.A7.E7.99.BE.E7.A7.91.E4.BA.8B.E5.85.B8.20.E7.AC.AC.EF.BC.92.E7.89.88|title=Monzen Town|publisher=[[Sekai Daijiten]] (World Encyclopedia) 2nd Edition / Kotobank|access-date=March 7, 2018}}</ref> Larger-scale examples are sometimes defined as {{ill|Religious Cities|en|宗教都市}} or {{ill|Temple Precinct Cities|en|境内都市}}. Historically, cityscapes often developed near castles, harbors, markets, and religious institutions.

Monzen towns form when shrine or temple workers and merchants serving pilgrims gather in front of large shrines or temples attracting many visitors. However, the establishment of monzen towns is not guaranteed by the scale of the religious institution, as certain temples historically prohibited public access.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.worldtimes.co.jp/japan/20221119-166722/ |title=70-Year-Old Thatch Roof Repair of Jionji Main Hall, a National Important Cultural Property in Yamagata Prefecture's Sagae City|publisher=The World Times|date=November 19, 2022|access-date=May 26, 2023}}</ref>

Regions that still retain historical elements as monzen towns are designated as {{ill|Important Preservation Districts for Groups of Traditional Buildings|en|重要伝統的建造物群保存地区}}, with preservation measures enacted. Since 1998, the {{ill|National Monzen Town Summit|en|全国門前町サミット}} has been held annually to promote exchanges between such towns.

== Monzen Towns and Torii-mae Towns in Important Preservation Districts ==

* [[Sakamoto (Ōtsu City)|Sakamoto]] - {{ill|Shiga Prefecture|en|滋賀県}}, {{ill|Ōtsu City|en|大津市}} - {{ill|Hiyoshi Taisha|en|日吉大社}}, {{ill|Enryakuji|en|延暦寺}} (categorized as "Monzen Town and Sato-bo Group")
* [[Sagatoriimoto]] - {{ill|Kyoto Prefecture|en|京都府}}, {{ill|Kyoto City|en|京都市}}, {{ill|Ukyo Ward|en|右京区}} - {{ill|Atago Shrine|en|愛宕神社}}
* [[Sannenzaka]] - Kyoto City, {{ill|Higashiyama Ward|en|東山区}} - {{ill|Kiyomizu-dera|en|清水寺}}

