{{Otheruses|A type of historical town|The former municipality in Hōsu District, Ishikawa Prefecture (now Wajima City)|{{ill|Monzen Town (Ishikawa)|ja|門前町 (石川県)}}|For other uses, see {{ill|Monzen Town (disambiguation)|ja|門前町 (曖昧さ回避)}}.}}{{Otheruses|A type of historical town|A former municipality in Hōsu District, Ishikawa Prefecture, now part of Wajima City|Monzen Town (Ishikawa)|Other uses|Monzen Town (disambiguation)}}
{{Cite sources clearly|date=June 8, 2015 (Mon) 05:51 (UTC)}}
[[File:Sakamoto Otsu03s4592.jpg|thumb|Sakamoto]]
[[File:Toriimoto03s3200.jpg|thumb|Sagatoriimoto]]
[[File:Stone stairway Kiyomizu-dera.JPG|thumb|150px|{{ill|Sanneizaka|ja|産寧坂}}]]
'''Monzen Town''' (門前町, monzen-machi) refers to a town formed around prominent {{ill|Buddhist temples|ja|寺院}} or {{ill|Shinto shrines|ja|神社}}. Specifically, for shrines, it is often called a '''Torii-mae Town''' (鳥居前町, torii-mae-machi).

== Overview ==
Broadly speaking, settlements formed by followers of temples or shrines, including {{ill|shaji towns|ja|社家町}} and {{ill|jinai towns|ja|寺内町}}, are also considered Monzenmachi.<ref>
{{Cite web|和書|url=https://kotobank.jp/word/%E9%96%80%E5%89%8D%E7%94%BA-143199#E4.B8.96.E7.95.8C.E5.A4.A7.E7.99.BE.E7.A7.91.E4.BA.8B.E5.85.B8.20.E7.AC.AC.EF.BC.92.E7.89.88|title=Monzenmachi|publisher={{ill|World Encyclopedia|ja|世界大百科事典}} 2nd Edition / Kotobank|accessdate=2018-03-07}}
</ref> Larger-scale examples may be defined as {{ill|religious cities|ja|宗教都市}} or {{ill|precinct cities|ja|境内都市}}. Historically, sites for urban formation included castles, ports, markets, and exchange hubs, with temples and shrines being among these.

Monzenmachi typically developed in front of prominent shrines or temples that attracted many worshippers, creating hubs for commerce and crafts to cater to both shrine officials and visitors. However, such towns were not always formed even with large temples, as some temples restricted public access.<ref>{{Cite web|和書|url=https://www.worldtimes.co.jp/japan/20221119-166722/|title=Comprehensive repair of thatched roofs after 70 years: The national important cultural property "Main Hall of Jionji" in Sagae City, Yamagata Prefecture|publisher=The World Times|date=2022-11-19|accessdate=2023-05-26}}</ref>

Districts retaining historical features as Monzenmachi are designated as {{ill|Important Preservation Districts for Groups of Traditional Buildings|ja|重要伝統的建造物群保存地区}} and preserved accordingly. Exchanges between Monzenmachi towns have also taken place, with the {{ill|National Monzenmachi Summit|ja|全国門前町サミット}} being held annually since 1998.

== Overview ==

In a broad sense, the term includes settlements formed by shrine or temple followers, such as shaji towns and {{ill|Jinaimachi|ja|寺内町}}.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://kotobank.jp/word/%E9%96%80%E5%89%8D%E7%94%BA-143199#E4.B8.96.E7.95.8C.E5.A4.A7.E7.99.BE.E7.A7.91.E4.BA.8B.E5.85.B8.20.E7.AC.AC.EF.BC.92.E7.89.88|title=Monzen Town|publisher=[[Sekai Daijiten]] (World Encyclopedia) 2nd Edition / Kotobank|access-date=March 7, 2018}}</ref> Larger-scale examples are sometimes defined as {{ill|Religious Cities|ja|宗教都市}} or {{ill|Temple Precinct Cities|ja|境内都市}}. Historically, cityscapes often developed near castles, harbors, markets, and religious institutions.

Monzen towns form when shrine or temple workers and merchants serving pilgrims gather in front of large shrines or temples attracting many visitors. However, the establishment of monzen towns is not guaranteed by the scale of the religious institution, as certain temples historically prohibited public access.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.worldtimes.co.jp/japan/20221119-166722/ |title=70-Year-Old Thatch Roof Repair of Jionji Main Hall, a National Important Cultural Property in Yamagata Prefecture's Sagae City|publisher=The World Times|date=November 19, 2022|access-date=May 26, 2023}}</ref>

Regions that still retain historical elements as monzen towns are designated as {{ill|Important Preservation Districts for Groups of Traditional Buildings|ja|重要伝統的建造物群保存地区}}, with preservation measures enacted. Since 1998, the {{ill|National Monzen Town Summit|ja|全国門前町サミット}} has been held annually to promote exchanges between such towns.

== Monzen Towns and Torii-mae Towns in Important Preservation Districts ==

